00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:01.490 align:start position:0%
 
in the United States the government's  top infectious disease expert has  sounded the alarm about the rapid spread  of the corona virus dr. Anthony Fauci  says that the number of daily cases  could more than double if the country  fails to contain a surge in infections  across several states another bleak  warning from the experts speaking to  lawmakers in Washington the message  couldn't be clearer I'm very concerned  about what's going on right now  particularly in the four states that are  accounting for about 50% of the new  infections but the other vulnerable  States so I'd have to say the numbers  speak for themselves I'm very concerned  and I'm not satisfied with what's going  on because we're going in the wrong  direction the states he's referring to a  Florida Arizona Texas and California  they've seen infection surge forcing  governor's to wind back efforts to  reopen their economies states that bore  the brunt of the virus early and making  quarantine mandatory for new arrivals  are vulnerable divided between former  and current virus hotspots we can't just  focus on those areas that are having the  surge it puts the entire country at risk  we are now having 40 plus thousand new  cases a day I would not be surprised if  we go up to a hundred thousand a day if  this does not turn around  dr. fat cheese was just one in a chorus  of concerned voices who say not enough  Americans are social distancing or  wearing masks unfortunately this simple  life-saving practice has become part of  the political debate that says this if  you're for Trump you don't wear a mask  him you're against Trump you do that's  why I've suggested that the president  occasionally wear a mask the Republican  urged the president to set a better  example for many the stakes are too high  for any political stigma surrounding  masks  from the u.s. to the Czech Republic now  where people have been celebrating a  perceived end to the coronavirus crisis  in their country with a massive public  dinner party in the capital now large  crowds gathered on Prague's charles  bridge on tuesday and sat down to dine  at a gigantic 500 meter table the  country has by now eased most  restrictions which had been introduced  fairly early on in the pandemic the  Czech Republic registered fewer than  12,000 coronavirus cases and under 350  deaths brazil's army has been testing  isolated indigenous communities for the  coronavirus on this visit no one tested  positive but the pandemic is threatening  to decimate hundreds of Amazon tribes  they have a little immunity to external  diseases and have a communal lifestyle  that rules out social distancing  authorities in northwestern Germany are  preparing to ease the strict to lockdown  imposed in some districts after a kovat  19 outbreak flared at a beet processing  factory their people have been ordered  to stay in their homes and are banned  from traveling anywhere for many of  those who have seen quarantine returned  patience is wearing thin but testing who  is healthy and who is not is a major  challenge these employees of the Tania's  meat processing plant and other  residents want out about 650 people have  been restricted to this block on the  outskirts of Farrell a city of 25,000 in  western Germany they've been placed  under quarantine but this is set to  change the mayor is preparing to lift  the quarantine gradually but he still  faces one big obstacle trying to figure  out who is who and who is healthy there  are different lists of people who are  actually living there and people who  should be there but probably left before  the quarantine who've likely headed home  I met many in feral and in the  neighboring larger city of Gutierrez lo  also want out after all  it's summer vacation but numerous German  holiday resorts will only accept  travelers from high-risk locations if  they've tested negative for the  coronavirus this temporary testing  facility is being run with the help of  soldiers from the German army every day  we test over 700 people here honestly  we've yet to see a positive test that's  some good news for a change  good news but people here are still  angry especially at the management of  the Chinese meat plant which was ground  zero for a fresh outbreak of the  pandemic in this region it's their fault  the meat plant acted incredibly  irresponsibly the police and the city  need to come down hard on them for now  the city has its hands full it has to  decide who may and may not be released  from quarantine workers have to check  people against lists of those who've  been tested it's still going to take  quite some time weeks maybe months until  we're done with everything it will  likely also take a long time until we  finally have a vaccine or drugs to treat  the illness until then  local residents will have to live with  lockdown restrictions and here are some  of the other developments in the corona  virus pandemic the German state of  Bavaria is to bring in universal testing  for Cova 19 it's prompted a debate about  whether the rest of Germany should  follow suit or stick to targeted testing  to prevent a possible second wave  coronavirus deaths will soon top 60,000  in brazil the worst kit hit country  after the United States the army has  delivered protective supplies and  medicines by helicopter to indigenous  communities in the Amazon Thailand has  begun a fifth phase of relaxations of  restrictions schools and high-risk  entertainment venues such as bars and  massage parlors have been allowed to  reopen and China has reported just three  new cases of corona virus compared to 19  the day before all the new cases were in  Beijing the EU has opened its external  borders to leisure and business travel  today but not to all countries  oh no virus hotspots including the US  and Russia were excluded from the  so-called  safe list of countries whose residents  can enter the bloc Australia Japan and  South Korea did make the cut however  Chinese travelers can also enter but  only after China opens its own borders  it's a difficult summer for tourism  dependent businesses in Europe with more  EU residents staying close to home  Chelsey Delaney is on the story for us  she joins us now from Frankfurt so what  is the reopening mean for the EU  well this reopening is really crucial  for the European economy as you said  there are a lot of countries that are  extremely dependent on on travelers from  abroad so especially countries like  Greece or Italy they they get a lot of  their their revenue from trip from  travel especially in this summer travel  season so the lack of travel has really  impacted a lot of people and a lot of  businesses but this is also a bit of a  disappointment for those dependent  people because this doesn't include the  United States which about a 15 million  people travel from the u.s. to to the EU  every year so that's a big loss of  revenue and it also doesn't include  Brazil Russia countries like that so  it's not a total relief for some of  these from for some of these businesses  you are at the Frankfurt Airport right  now what precautions are being taken  there  so a big part of the the safety measures  here at the airport are everyone has to  wear a mask there are disinfecting  stations located at throughout the  airport but a lot of it is really  signage so there are a lot of signs  there are a lot of announcements every  few minutes saying please wear a mask  there aren't there aren't really any  things like temperature checks things  like that that we've seen at other  airports they do have two testing  centers here where you can get tested  for corona virus one of them opened  earlier this week but you do have to pay  for them yourself and you also have to  wait a little while to get the results  so I think it's written in general a  pretty light a level of safety measures  here at the airport  Chelsea Delaney in Frankfort thank you

